note.text:
Effects of the migrations:
• Cameroon became home to over 250 ethnic groups and languages, which is why it is called “Africa in miniature”;
• peoples mixed through marriage, trade and conquest, and some were pushed into forests or mountains;
• new states were formed: the Fulani lamidates (Ngaoundéré, Garoua, Maroua), the Bamum kingdom, the Grassfields chiefdoms;
• new religions and cultures spread (Islam in the north);
• conflicts over land and pasture, but also cooperation.
Sources for studying migrations: oral traditions (stories of origin), languages (related words), archaeology, and written accounts by Arab and European travellers.
